Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 10:47:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 055/154] fix: stop the sidebar position crashing the browser,
 and wire the settings that reached nothing
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The settings surface rendered real values, persisted real edits, and applied
none of them. LoadSettingsJSON had exactly one caller: the surface pushing
values back to its own page.

- Warn before quitting reaches Chrome's confirm-to-quit pref. Proven by
  sending the same chord twice in one launch with only the toggle between
  them, and grading that the app went on serving afterwards.
- Sidebar position, opaque surround, glass opacity and sidebar width apply
  through one seam, on a successful write and on a startup seed that reads
  each store once.
- Startup behaviour reaches the launch plan. Swift gated the startup tab on
  it and the port kept the computation while dropping the gate.
- hotkeys.json is installed. It would have refused every payload anyway:
  the reader demanded schema 1 while the core emits 2.

A stored sidebar position of "right" crashed the browser before any window
existed. Not a half-built window, as first assumed: sidebar_host_ is the
deleted SwiftUI arm's view host, permanently null since the Swift package
went, so that function was a guaranteed null dereference for any caller. It
had no caller for months. The accessor is gone now, so a fourth occurrence
is a compile error rather than a fourth silent no-op.

Cmd+Q reaches the menu again from the settings window. A surface holding
first responder claims the chord, because CommandDispatcher only calls
postPerformKeyEquivalent when the view hierarchy declines and a live render
widget never does.

Three command decisions moved to the Rust core; ABI 6 to 9 across the night.
236 C++ tests, checked by name rather than by count after finding two suites
the default filter silently skipped.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
